      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Splitting a line in a ratio"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A flag is to be fixed one-third of the way from one corner of a field to the other. Coordinates and the section formula tell us the exact point."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "(7, 3)",
          "centre = mid-point",
          "internal division only",
          "m1 with B",
          "mid-point",
          "ratio m1 : m2",
          "section formula",
          "unknown ratio"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Suppose a point P divides the segment joining A(x1, y1) and B(x2, y2) internally in the ratio m1 : m2.\nThen the coordinates of P are (m1 x2 + m2 x1) by (m1 + m2), and (m1 y2 + m2 y1) by (m1 + m2).\nHere AP : PB = m1 : m2. Be careful: m1 goes with the coordinates of B.\nThe mid-point is the special case with ratio 1 : 1, so it is ((x1 + x2)/2, (y1 + y2)/2).\nFind the point dividing (4, -3) and (8, 5) in the ratio 3 : 1. x = (3 x 8 + 1 x 4)/4 = 7 and y = (3 x 5 + 1 x (-3))/4 = 3.\nTo find the ratio, let P divide in k : 1 and use the formula to solve for k.\nThe centre of a circle is the mid-point of a diameter, a useful special case.\nExternal division is not in this syllabus; it is met in higher classes."
        }
      },
